    "function": "zend_jit_trace",
    "file": "php-src/ext/opcache/jit/zend_jit_trace.c",
    "description": "In the loop-header register-allocation/phi setup, the `ival->flags & ZREG_LOAD` and `info & MAY_BE_GUARD` path calls `SET_STACK_TYPE(stack, i, concrete_type(info), 1)` (around line 4109). Here `i` is stale from the preceding initialization loop and equals `last_var`; the intended index is `phi->var`, as used by the immediately following `SET_STACK_REG_EX` and load. This writes past the trace compiler's abstract stack map, corrupting trace/deoptimization metadata and causing generated JIT code to return to the VM with an invalid frame/operand base. The eventual wild read in `zval_get_type()` is downstream when the ADD handler computes `EX_VAR()` from that corrupted state.",
    "conditions": "Tracing JIT and register allocation must be enabled, and compilation must produce a loop (or recursive loop-like trace) with a phi whose lifetime interval has `ZREG_LOAD` and whose inferred type still has `MAY_BE_GUARD`. The PoC's hot loop, changing/undefined arithmetic variables, and reference aliasing (`$a =& $a`) create this guarded loaded phi. During trace compilation the stale index performs the out-of-bounds abstract-stack write; when that trace executes/exits to the generic `ZEND_ADD_SPEC_TMPVARCV_TMPVARCV_HANDLER`, its corrupted execution state yields wild `op1`/`op2` pointers and crashes in `add_function_fast`/`zval_get_type`."
